{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/b1-explainer-about-old-emoji-and-emoticons-that-people-still-use-in-other-countries-how-em/audio/main-4f8ffb63c572.mp3","durationSec":266.592,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":3.94,"text":"Before emoji became common, people made faces from keyboard symbols."},{"start":4.52,"end":6.16,"text":"These are called emoticons."},{"start":7.12,"end":12.82,"text":"A face such as :-) is read sideways, while XD shows strong laughter."},{"start":13.6,"end":17.3,"text":"They became popular in early email, forums, and chat rooms."},{"start":18.06,"end":20.42,"text":"They may look old now, but they have not disappeared."},{"start":21.28,"end":29.36,"text":"Emoticons that people still use in other countries include the Russian closing bracket, XD in gaming chats, and <3 in nostalgic messages."},{"start":30.48,"end":32.9,"text":"Some habits developed in particular online communities."},{"start":33.78,"end":38.58,"text":"In Russian-language messages, a person may write one closing bracket, ), to show friendliness."},{"start":39.38,"end":42.92,"text":"Several brackets, ))), can suggest stronger warmth or laughter."},{"start":44.24,"end":50.42,"text":"In other countries, XD and <3 are still used in gaming communities or nostalgic posts."},{"start":51.2,"end":56.36,"text":"An old face can feel playful, sincere, or unfashionable, depending on who sends it."},{"start":56.36,"end":59.48,"text":"Modern emoji grew from Japanese mobile communication."},{"start":60.32,"end":69.42,"text":"NTT DoCoMo released an influential set in 1999, and international Unicode support later helped emoji spread across devices and countries."},{"start":70.22,"end":73.9,"text":"However, the pictures travelled more easily than their original meanings."},{"start":74.78,"end":79.86,"text":"For example, 🙏 can express thanks, a request, or an apology in Japan."},{"start":80.6,"end":85.42,"text":"Elsewhere, people may understand it mainly as prayer, and some users call it a high five."},{"start":86.92,"end":89.68,"text":"The face 😤 also shows change."},{"start":91.06,"end":100.66,"text":"Its steam originally suggested triumph or determination in a Japanese visual tradition, but many international users now read it as anger or frustration."},{"start":101.58,"end":106.4,"text":"Meaning therefore comes from both the picture’s source and the new setting where people use it."},{"start":107.22,"end":111.42,"text":"A kaomoji is a text face that is usually read upright, not sideways."},{"start":111.92,"end":121.7,"text":"Examples include (^_^) for happiness, (T_T) for crying, and (¬_¬) for doubt or annoyance."},{"start":122.78,"end":128.1,"text":"These faces became strongly connected with Japanese online culture, although people now use them internationally."},{"start":129.18,"end":131.46,"text":"Their design also shows an interesting contrast."},{"start":132.62,"end":138.66,"text":"In many Western emoticons, the mouth carries the main emotion: compare :-) with :-(."},{"start":140.56,"end":143.7,"text":"In kaomoji, the eyes often do more of the work."},{"start":144.48,"end":147.34,"text":"The rest of the face can add arms, tears, or movement."},{"start":148.26,"end":153.66,"text":"The international shrug is a small text picture made of bars, a round mouth, and raised arms."},{"start":154.4,"end":158.7,"text":"It carries a simple message: “I do not know” or “What can you do?”"},{"start":159.72,"end":162.58,"text":"Stickers are larger and more detailed than ordinary emoji."},{"start":163.34,"end":166.92,"text":"They may show a full character, include words, or move through animation."},{"start":167.44,"end":174.52,"text":"Because apps sell or provide different sticker collections, local messaging habits can develop around particular characters."},{"start":174.52,"end":178.96,"text":"LINE stickers are especially familiar in Japan, Thailand, and Taiwan."},{"start":178.96,"end":186.24,"text":"LINE characters such as Brown and Cony began as messaging stickers and later appeared on many physical products."},{"start":186.8,"end":197.02,"text":"In South Korea, KakaoTalk has its own popular character stickers, while WeChat users in China often exchange animated images and user-made sticker collections."},{"start":198.12,"end":202.64,"text":"A sticker can soften a refusal, show thanks, or end a conversation warmly."},{"start":203.16,"end":209.28,"text":"However, someone outside that app community may not recognize the character or understand the social tone."},{"start":210.16,"end":213.04,"text":"There is no perfect international emoji dictionary."},{"start":213.64,"end":219.78,"text":"A useful method is to consider three things: the person, the platform, and the situation."},{"start":220.72,"end":222.72,"text":"First, age and relationship matter."},{"start":222.72,"end":229.8,"text":"A simple 🙂 may seem pleasantly polite to one person but cold or passive-aggressive to another."},{"start":229.8,"end":236.38,"text":"Second, platforms sometimes display the same emoji with slightly different designs."},{"start":236.86,"end":240.46,"text":"A small change in the eyes or mouth can change its emotional effect."},{"start":241.28,"end":244.44,"text":"Finally, online communities create new meanings."},{"start":245.06,"end":254.34,"text":"In many English-language social media spaces, 💀 can mean “That was so funny I died,” rather than actual danger or death."},{"start":255.2,"end":258.34,"text":"Old faces can also return as jokes or signs of nostalgia."},{"start":259.24,"end":266.04,"text":"When meaning is unclear, the surrounding message and the sender’s usual style are often more useful than the symbol alone."}],"words":[{"text":"Before","start":0,"end":0.36},{"text":"emoji","start":0.36,"end":0.68},{"text":"became","start":0.68,"end":1.02},{"text":"common","start":1.02,"end":1.46},{"text":"people","start":1.78,"end":2.04},{"text":"made","start":2.04,"end":2.3},{"text":"faces","start":2.3,"end":2.66},